"""
Reproducing spec — `MirLowering.global_statics_by_id` / `global_constants_by_id`
must be initialised, not nil.

Record: doc/08_tracking/bug/mcp_native_build_post_mono_nil_contains_2026-09-01.md

Both fields are DECLARED in src/compiler/50.mir/mir_lowering_types.spl:424-425
as `Dict<i64, MirStatic>` / `Dict<i64, MirConstant>`, and until 2026-09-02 they
were initialised NOWHERE: absent from the only constructor call
(_MirLowering/module_lowering.spl:253 `MirLowering.new_for_target`), and the
only two assignments in the tree (_MirLoweringExpr/switch_operators_calls.spl
:4468-4469) merely copy `self`'s already-nil value into a sub-lowerer. So every
MirLowering carried them as `Value::Nil` for its whole life, and the first read

    me find_global_static(symbol_id: i64) -> MirStatic?:
        if self.global_statics_by_id.contains(symbol_id):     # <-- fatal

(_MirLoweringExpr/expr_dispatch.spl:223) aborted the build with

    error: semantic: method `contains` not found on type `nil` (receiver value: nil)

This is what stopped the MCP native build (src/app/mcp/main.spl). Contrary to
the record's original wording it dies INSIDE MIR lowering, not between mono and
MIR entry — the seed's `[mnf-debug-spl]` stack names the frame chain
    ... -> lower_to_mir -> lower_module -> lower_function -> lower_block
        -> lower_expr -> lower_expr_impl -> try_lower_global_read
        -> find_global_static
and `[mnf-expr]` names the receiver `__nested_field_global_statics_by_id__`.

This spec pins the LANGUAGE-level contract that made the defect invisible:
omitting a `Dict` field from a constructor call leaves it nil rather than an
empty dict, so the mistake is silent at construction and only fatal at the
first read. Measured on seed src/compiler_rust/target/release/simple.exe
md5 286f66b8615dce0e0da788f0550c4008, SIMPLE_EXECUTION_MODE=interpret.

NOTE for future readers: mir_lowering_types.spl:405-412 claims that naming such
a field in the constructor STILL leaves it nil, and recommends a bool-set
workaround. That claim does NOT reproduce on this seed for a struct-valued
`Dict<i64, Struct>` field — see the "constructor-initialised field" examples
below, which pass. The real defect was omission, not a broken initialiser.
"""
use std.spec.{describe, it, expect}

struct Payload:
    name: text

struct Holder:
    a: Dict<i64, bool>
    b: Dict<i64, Payload>
    c: Dict<i64, Payload>

impl Holder:
    # The fixed shape: every Dict field named in the constructor call.
    static fn initialized() -> Holder:
        Holder(a: {}, b: {}, c: {})

    me probe_empty() -> bool:
        self.b.contains(1)

    me store_then_read() -> text:
        self.b[7] = Payload(name: "seven")
        self.b[7].name

    me store_then_contains() -> bool:
        self.c[3] = Payload(name: "three")
        self.c.contains(3)

    me len_after_two_stores() -> i64:
        self.b[1] = Payload(name: "one")
        self.b[2] = Payload(name: "two")
        self.b.len()

describe "MirLowering's global lookup maps must be constructed, not left nil":

    it "a constructor-initialised struct-valued Dict answers .contains() as empty":
        var h = Holder.initialized()
        expect(h.probe_empty()).to_equal(false)

    it "a constructor-initialised struct-valued Dict accepts an index-assign and reads back":
        var h = Holder.initialized()
        expect(h.store_then_read()).to_equal("seven")

    it "a constructor-initialised struct-valued Dict answers .contains() after a store":
        var h = Holder.initialized()
        expect(h.store_then_contains()).to_equal(true)

    it "a constructor-initialised struct-valued Dict reports its length":
        var h = Holder.initialized()
        expect(h.len_after_two_stores()).to_equal(2)
